				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kimura-okagawa" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(398761); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=398761&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973756" alt="Kimura Okagawa" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=398761)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kimura-okagawa" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(398761); return false;" >Kimura Okagawa</a></span> <span class="odate time_1264907684 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">31 Jan 2010 03:14</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-688152">
									

<p>Wow. So that's what happens when an SCP gets BSoD'd… Better make a note to never click on infectous bacteria then.</p>
<p>Here's a test idea: What happens if someone is put into the computer, <em>but not in the Recycle Bin</em>, and then taken out? Would they still lose a small part of their body like test 82, or would they just return whole? In other words, maybe only the Recycle Bin itself causes "data" loss, rather than the very act of "digitization", to use a certain term…</p>
<p>Oh, another idea. Drag someone onto the desktop (not in the Recycle Bin), copy their file, then drag both files offscreen. Easiest. Cloning. Ever. Assuming it works, SCP-713's file-copy command could be a D-class generator.</p>

							</div>

								
					
